From e972f824ecb3a6ec2b1c90df315e82afcbe48ba6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kbench <kbench@localhost>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 11:34:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] ipv4: don't generate an IP ID for connected atomic
 datagrams

ip_select_ident_segs() tests "is there a connected socket" before it
tests IP_DF, so a connected UDP socket sending a DF datagram takes

	val = atomic_add_return(segs, &inet_sk(sk)->inet_id);

and puts the result in a field nothing may use. The datagram is atomic:
DF is set, so it cannot be fragmented and nothing can reassemble it.
RFC 6864 2.1 leaves the field to the sender in that case and requires
receivers to ignore it. Three lines further down, the same function
already sends zero for an atomic datagram with no connected socket.

Test IP_DF first on that path and send zero, matching what the
unconnected case does today. The atomic RMW disappears, and with it the
only write this send makes to that cacheline -- which matters most when
several threads share one socket, the shape a QUIC client has.

TCP is deliberately left alone. It does not reach the atomic: it holds
the socket lock and uses a plain read and write, so there is nothing to
save, and its IDs are more useful than the nothing this would buy.

The change is visible on the wire -- the ID goes from a counter to zero
on connected DF datagrams -- so passive fingerprinting of such senders
changes. It does not become distinguishable from other Linux traffic,
because that is already what unconnected DF sends look like.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XVxrZjHzxnmyA7MDozho8A
---
 include/net/ip.h | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/include/net/ip.h b/include/net/ip.h
index 6f602df72ee6..d9c5bb9d471d 100644
--- a/include/net/ip.h
+++ b/include/net/ip.h
@@ -597,6 +597,21 @@ static inline void ip_select_ident_segs(struct net *net, struct sk_buff *skb,
 			sock_owned_by_me(sk);
 			val = atomic_read(&inet_sk(sk)->inet_id);
 			atomic_set(&inet_sk(sk)->inet_id, val + segs);
+		} else if ((iph->frag_off & htons(IP_DF)) && !skb->ignore_df) {
+			/* An atomic datagram carries no meaningful ID: nothing
+			 * may reassemble it, so RFC 6864 2.1 leaves the field
+			 * to the sender and requires receivers to ignore it.
+			 * The unconnected path a few lines below already sends
+			 * zero here; match it, and skip the atomic RMW on
+			 * inet_id, which is otherwise the only write this send
+			 * makes to that cacheline.
+			 *
+			 * TCP keeps its counter: it does not pay the atomic
+			 * anyway, having the socket lock, and its IDs are worth
+			 * more than the nothing this saves there.
+			 */
+			iph->id = 0;
+			return;
 		} else {
 			val = atomic_add_return(segs, &inet_sk(sk)->inet_id);
 		}
